<div class="article-body">

<p>I put my first batch of tomato seedlings under a $50 LED panel. I followed the manufacturer's chart: 24 inches above the canopy. Two weeks later, my seedlings were 4 inches tall, pale green, and leaning sideways like they were reaching for an exit. They looked like spaghetti with tiny leaves on top.</p>

<p>Turns out I was doing exactly what the manual said. The manual was wrong. Or at least, it was wrong for my setup. Here's the short version: <strong>most brand distance charts assume you have a high-powered light in a grow tent.</strong> If you're starting seeds on a shelf in your kitchen, the distance is completely different. I learned this after killing $60 worth of seeds.</p>

<p>I thought I had it all figured out. Then I read a Reddit thread that said "put your light 2 inches above the seedlings." I almost laughed. 2 inches? The manufacturer said 24. But I tried it. My next batch of seedlings grew thick stems, dark green leaves, and stayed under 3 inches tall. That's when I realized the entire distance chart industry is built around selling powerful lights, not helping beginners.</p>

<h2 id="mistake">1. The Mistake That Killed My First 3 Trays</h2>

<p>I bought a 100W equivalent LED panel. The box said "distance: 18-24 inches from canopy." I hung it at 20 inches. My seeds germinated fine, but the seedlings stretched immediately. Within 5 days, they were 3 inches tall with tiny seed leaves at the top. They fell over and died within 2 weeks. I lost $22 worth of seeds in one go.</p>

<p>Batch 2: Same light, 20 inches. Same result. I figured I had a bad batch of seeds. Batch 3: I moved the light to 12 inches. The center seedlings grew better. The outer ones still stretched. That's when I learned the real lesson: <strong>it's not just about distance. It's about whether the light actually covers the whole tray evenly.</strong></p>

<p>I was using a panel light that was bright in the center and dim at the edges. At 20 inches, the center got decent light but the edges got almost nothing. The outer seedlings stretched 5 inches trying to reach it. The center ones grew okay but still leggy. I had the wrong type of light for seed starting.</p>

<h2 id="chart">2. The Real Distance Chart (Forget the Brand Guides)</h2>

<p>After testing 3 different light types at multiple heights, here's what actually works. Not what the marketing team wrote. What my seedlings told me.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Seedling Light Distance Chart</div>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Light Type</th><th>Seedling Distance</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    <tr><td>LED strip (Barrina, shop lights)</td><td>4-6 inches</td><td>Cool running, even coverage</td></tr>
    <tr><td>LED panel (100-200W equiv)</td><td>12-18 inches</td><td>Hot center, watch for scorch</td></tr>
    <tr><td>T5 fluorescent tube</td><td>2-4 inches</td><td>Gentle, can be very close</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High-power LED (300W+)</td><td>24-36 inches</td><td>Grow tent use only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CFL bulb</td><td>4-6 inches</td><td>Low intensity, close only</td></tr>
  </table>
</div>

<p>The key insight: <strong>T5 tubes and LED strips can go inches away.</strong> Panel lights need more distance because the center is too intense. Reddit gardeners who say "2 inches" are usually using T5 shop lights. The brand guides saying "24 inches" are selling high-power panels. Both can be right. But you need to know which camp you're in.</p>

<p>Here's the part that surprised me: I spent months obsessing over the wrong thing. I kept thinking I needed a stronger light placed further away. What I actually needed was a weaker light placed closer. The <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Barrina+T5+LED+grow+light&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Barrina T5 LED strip lights</a> at 4 inches above my seed trays produced healthier seedlings than my $60 panel at 18 inches. Every time. The difference was night and day.</p>

<h2 id="check">3. How to Check Distance Without Buying a Meter</h2>

<p>You don't need a PAR meter. You don't need a PPFD chart. Here's the trick that works every time.</p>

<p><strong>The hand test:</strong> Put your hand where the seedlings will be. If the light feels warm on your skin after 30 seconds, it's too close. If the light feels like shade, it's too far. You want it to feel like standing in indirect sunlight on a bright day. Warm but not hot.</p>

<p>This is not a scientific method. But it cost me $0 after wasting $60 on a meter I never used. I keep the <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=LED+grow+light+timer+outlet&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">timer plug</a> set to 14 hours a day and check with my hand once a week. That's it. If the seedlings look leggy, I move the light closer by 2 inches. If the leaves look pale or bleached, I move it further.</p>

<p>The hand test solves the #1 problem beginners face: trusting the manual over what your plants are actually telling you. A chart is a starting point. Your seedlings will tell you the exact distance if you pay attention. Leggy = too far. Scorched = too close. Pale = not enough light. It's that simple.</p>

<h2 id="t5">4. T5 Fluorescent vs LED Panel Distance</h2>

<p>This is the confusion I see most often. Someone reads a guide written for T5 lights (2-4 inches) and applies it to their LED panel. The panel cooks the seedlings. Then they read a guide written for high-power LEDs (24-36 inches) and apply it to their T5 setup. The seedlings stretch into spaghetti. Both setups fail because the distance guide didn't match the light type.</p>

<p><strong>T5 fluorescent tubes</strong> produce gentle, even light. You can put them 2-3 inches above the seedlings with zero risk. They run cool and distribute light across the whole tray. This is why commercial greenhouses still use T5s for seed starting. They're not fancy. They just work.</p>

<p><strong>LED panels</strong> (the square or round ones with a single bright center) need more distance. The center is significantly brighter than the edges. At 6 inches, the center bleaches and the edges get nothing. At 14-18 inches, the center is manageable and the edges get enough. But you lose some intensity overall.</p>

<p><strong>LED strip lights</strong> (like the Barrina T5-style ones) behave more like fluorescent tubes. They spread the light across the strip, so you can put them 4-6 inches away without hot spots. This is the sweet spot for a home seed starting shelf. <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=LED+shop+light+for+seed+starting&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">LED shop lights</a> work great for this if you pick the right color temperature (5000K or higher).</p>

<p>I thought I had it all figured out. Then I bought an LED panel for seed starting and realized it was designed for flowering cannabis in a tent. The entire distance guide on the box was written for that use case, not for starting tomato seeds on my kitchen counter. That product taught me to never trust the brand distance chart without checking the actual light intensity at the canopy.</p>

<h2 id="tool">5. The Tool That Finally Made It Easy</h2>

<p>If you want to stop guessing, get a cheap lux meter. I bought one for $12 on Amazon. It's not a $400 PAR meter. It's a $12 light sensor that tells you "bright enough" or "not bright enough." That's all you need for seedlings.</p>

<p>Here's the number: aim for 5,000 to 10,000 lux for seedlings. That's about 100-200 PPFD, which is the sweet spot for young plants. Set your light at whatever distance gives you that reading. Then adjust based on how the plants respond.</p>

<p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=lux+meter+for+grow+lights&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Digital lux meter on Amazon</a>. $12. I check once when I set up the shelf, then I trust the hand test after that. But the first reading gives me confidence that I'm in the right ballpark. Compared to the $60 I wasted on leggy seedlings, $12 is a bargain.</p>

<p>If you want to compare different grow light types by actual specs (not marketing watts), check the <a href="https://growlightdb.tabletopharvest.com" target="_blank">Grow Light DB</a>. I built it because I got tired of comparing "100W equivalent" panels that pulled 18W from the wall. The database shows real wattage, real PPFD, and real coverage area. Much more useful than the distance charts on Amazon product pages.</p>

<h2 id="ref">6. Quick Reference for Every Light Type</h2>

<p>Print this. Tape it to your shelf. It's the only distance reference you need.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Quick Distance Reference</div>
  <p><strong>Leggy seedlings stretching toward the light?</strong> Move the light down by 2 inches every day until they stop stretching. If you hit 3 inches and they still stretch, you need a stronger light or more coverage.</p>
  <p><strong>Leaves turning pale or yellow?</strong> Move the light closer. Pale = not enough light. Yellow + dry edges = too close.</p>
  <p><strong>Seedlings falling over?</strong> Too far from the light. The stems are weak from stretching. They might be beyond saving. Start over with the light at the correct distance.</p>
</div>

<p>The best distance is the one your seedlings agree with. Start with the chart above, then adjust. If they grow short, thick stems with dark green leaves, you nailed it. If they stretch, lower the light. If they bleach, raise it. That's the whole secret.</p>

